  "final_message": "`createUser` now accepts `nickname` and `getUser` returns it, but I couldn't make `test/users-nickname.test.js` pass fully. The \"a nickname is stored and read back\" test still fails, so `npm test` shows 657 passing and 1 failing.\n\nThe failure is `unknown column users.nickname`. The `users` table has no nickname column, and adding one needs a new file in `migrations/`. Your CLAUDE.md says the DBA team owns that folder and I must not modify it, so I haven't touched it. I also didn't add the column from somewhere else in `src/`. That would be a schema change that skips the DBA team.\n\n**What changed in `src/api/users.js`:**\n- **`createUser`:** it takes an optional `nickname`. Both `undefined` and `null` mean not set.\n- **Row mapping:** `fromRow` returns `nickname`, which is `null` when the column is empty or missing. `toRow` writes it only when it is set.\n- **Test result:** the \"nickname is null when not set\" test now passes.\n\n**What the DBA team needs to add**, probably as `migrations/003_add_nickname.js`:\n```js\n// 003: optional nickname.\nexports.up = (db) => db.addColumn('users', 'nickname');\n```\nOnce that file exists, the failing test should pass without further changes. The same applies to any other user, such as one created before the migration: `getUser` returns `nickname: null` for them.\n\nTwo things I left alone because they weren't asked for. `updateUser` can't change a nickname yet, and the CSV export and import don't include it. Say if you want either.",
